WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.480
It used to be thought that
being bilingual was a bad thing -

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.120
that it would confuse or hold
people back, especially children.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.760
Turns out,
we couldn't have been more wrong.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.240
Learning new languages
is an exercise of the mind.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.400
It's the mental equivalent of
going to a gym every day.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.680
In the bilingual brain,
all our languages are active,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.320
all at the same time.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.520
The continual effort
of suppressing a language

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.680
when speaking another, along with
the mental challenge

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.880
that comes with regularly switching
between languages,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.760
exercises our brain.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.360
It improves our concentration,
problem solving, memory,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.960
and in turn our creativity.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.640
It's now widely accepted
that there are huge benefits

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.120
to being bilingual.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.960
A key breakthrough came
back in 2007 in Toronto,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.760
when Ellen Bialystok and her team
made a discovery

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.080
that shook the
scientific community -

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.280
and has massive real-world
implications.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.240
It was the first
study which suggested

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.760
that bilingual people -

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.000
people who speak
more than one language -

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.360
develop dementia four
to four-and-a-half years later

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.240
than those who don't.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.720
It was a powerful confirmation
of the idea of cognitive reserve.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.320
Now, what is cognitive reserve?

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.840
Cognitive reserve is the idea
that people develop

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.520
a reserve of thinking abilities,
and this protects them

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.840
against losses that can occur
through ageing and disease.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.880
As well as delaying the onset
of dementia, bilingual people

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.160
have been shown to recover
significantly better after a stroke.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.440
Learning anything new helps build
cognitive reserve.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.840
But there's something
special about language.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.880
Language is particularly
broad and complex.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.240
It affects ideas and concepts,
perception, different sounds.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:03.000
The more complex a certain skill is,
the more likely it is

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.000
to have a positive
effect on cognitive reserve.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.480
So when is the best time
to learn a new language?

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.600
Well, here's part of the answer.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.520
The brain is a complex
set of neural networks.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.680
When you're learning a new
language as a child,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.160
you're building new networks.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.080
But when you learn
a language later in life,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.080
you have to modify
the existing networks

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.200
and make more connections.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.000
Because learning languages later
in life can be more challenging,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.200
the benefits can also be greater.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.680
But a 2023 study
at Great Ormond Street suggests

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.880
this is just part of the story.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.640
So we invited three groups
of children

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:57.840
that were aged eight to 10.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.600
We had a group of children who
were monolinguals.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.800
A group of children who had
early exposure

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.440
to Greek and English from birth -
they were our early bilinguals.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.480
And finally, we had a group who had
been exposed to English

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.720
between the age of two and five,
and they were our later bilinguals.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.880
So what we did that no-one
had done before,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.280
is that we asked the children to
lie in the scanner

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.400
while doing nothing -
and just stare at a cross.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.240
And during this,
we measured their brain activity.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.440
What we found that was really
exciting for us

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.840
is that our early bilingual group

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.160
had the strongest connectivity
in the network at rest.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.920
And these group of regions
are regions that light up

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.120
when we're doing nothing
and just mind-wandering.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.720
A little bit like if you're going
to the gym every day,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.200
your muscles might look
bigger at rest.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.200
Similarly your brain might be
better connected at rest,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.080
because you are learning
a language early.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.680
And this is something no-one
had found before.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.680
And there's more.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.360
One lesser-known behavioural effect

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.320
of bilingualism in both children
and adults

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.360
is the ability to see the other
people's perspective,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.640
or to understand that it is possible
to have different points of view.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.880
Recent studies have
also found that people tend to react

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.440
more emotionally in their first
language, and more rationally,

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.400
in a more abstract way,
in their second.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.920
And the way it is usually explained
is that the first language

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.480
is the one which we use to speak
with family, with friends -

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.200
in informal settings.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.360
The second language is usually
learned at school,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.160
at the university, at work.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.280
Scientists are discovering
new upsides to being bilingual

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.600
all the time.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.200
And it's not just our brains
that benefit.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.120
Learning new languages,

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.120
and speaking more than
one language,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.880
is very important - not only for
individuals, but also for societies.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.280
Learning new languages
can open doors

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.240
to new cultural experiences,
life opportunities.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.920
Different people,
different communities,

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.920
and different
ways of seeing the world.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.360
And with that,
we'll say a final...

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.840
Bye!

